creen to drive animation, not during gameplay? \"the frame counter (counts every frame)\" — should count always.\n\nLet me disassemble around the NMI to find INC. The NMI is at $8082. The mapping said the NMI's per-frame tail does frame timers: decrements $0747, $077F, $0780,X. And $804C INC $0774 is in Reset (once). Hmm, so maybe SMB doesn't have a global frame counter in RAM? But the task asserts one exists.\n\nActually wait — I recall in SMB, $0009 is not it. Hmm, let me search: SMB RAM $0774: \"FrameCounter\"? Hmm. But we saw $0774 = 0.\n\nLet me disassemble the NMI to look for an INC. The NMI at $8082 length 256. Let me disassemble $8082-$8090 to see. Actually easier: search PRG for INC instructions referencing a RAM address in the per-frame path. But the frame counter could be incremented in the NMI. Let me disassemble $8082 region.\n\nActually the mapping said $80F0-$8138 frame timers (decrements). Let me disassemble that region to see if there's an INC.\n\nAlternatively, trace_writes on $0774 for 60 frames would tell whether it's written per frame. The mapping says Reset INC's it. But during play, maybe nothing writes it.\n\nHmm wait, actually SMB frame counter: I now recall the variable `FrameCounter` in SMB is at $0774? No... Let me actually look. Hmm. There's a known address \"$0776 OperMode\" etc. And \"$0774\" ... Actually I'm not sure.\n\nLet me just empirically search. Use trace_writes to find a per-frame incrementing address? trace_writes requires knowing the address. \n\nAlternative approach: watch a large candidate set across frames is limited to 16. But I can watch 16 per call and do several calls — but each call is a new 60-frame run overlapping different time windows; the frame counter would show +1 steps in whichever set contains it.\n\nBut I need candidate sets. All 2048 addresses too many. But the frame counter must have changed between f0 and f1, so it's within the 53 changed. So candidate set is only 53! Great. I can watch them in 4 calls of 16. Let me do that. Actually I already watched 16 of them (some). Let me watch the remaining.\n\nFrom f0/f1 changed list (53 addresses):\n9, 245, 247, 248, 249, 770, 771, 772, 773, 774, 775, 1760, 1765, 1766, 1767, 1768, 1769, 1770, 1771, 1772, 1773, 1775, 1776, 1777, 1778, 1779, 1780, 1781, 1782, 1783, 1784, 1785, 1786, 1787, 1919, 1927, 1959, 1960, 1961, 1962, 1963, 1964, 1965, 1968, 1971, 1972, 1974, 1977, 1978, 1985, 1991, 2041, 2042.\n\nI already watched: 245, 770, 1919, 1927, 1960, 1964, 1965, 1972, 1974, 1977, 1978, 1991, 1908, 1909, 1910, 1911.\n\nRemaining: 9, 247, 248, 249, 771, 772, 773, 774, 775, 1760, 1765...1787, 1959, 1961, 1962, 1963, 1968, 1971, 1985, 2041, 2042.\n\nThat's 9,247,248,249,771,772,773,774,775 (9), 1760,1765,1766,1767,1768,1769,1770,1771,1772,1773,1775,1776,1777,... plenty. Let me watch 16 at a time, prioritizing low addresses and unusual ones.\n\nBut wait — the frame counter might not be in the changed set if the interval didn't cause a change... impossible; it always changes.\n\nAlso possible: the frame counter is a 2-byte value at e.g. $0009/$000A, and only $0009 changed.\n\nLet me watch set A: [9, 247, 248, 249, 771, 772, 773, 774, 775, 1760, 2041, 2042, 1959, 1961, 1962, 1963].\n\nIf the frame counter is among these, one will step by +1 each frame.\n\nLet me do that."}
